raight?\n- Only way to beat 6-7-8-9-10 is **7-8-9-10-J** or **8-9-10-J-Q** or **9-10-J-Q-K**, etc.\n\nBut J (Jack) is missing from the board. No Jack appears. So **no one can have a jack-high straight** unless they hold Jx.\n\nCould someone have a higher straight?\n- Only if they hold **J-8**, **J-5**, or **J with any**, then they\'d have **7-8-9-10-J** → Jack-high straight, which beats your ten-high straight.\n\nBut the board has no Jack. So for the opponent to have a straight, they must hold **J-8**, **J-5**, **J-6**, **J-7**, **J-9**, or **J-10** — specifically, they need a **Jack**.\n\nJ-x is required for a higher straight.\n\nOther straights:\n- Could someone have 5-8? Then 5-6-7-8-9 → nine-high straight, which is **lower** than yours (yours is ten-high).\n- Or 8-5? Same thing.\n\nSo:\n- Your straight (**6-7-8-9-10**) is the **second-nut** hand.\n- Only a **Jack and any card** (to make **7-8-9-10-J**) beats you.\n\nBut how likely is that?\n\nAlso, could someone have two pair or trips? Possibly, but unlikely given passive play.\n\nBoth players checked on the turn — suggesting caution.\n\nNow, since you’re first to act on the river (opponent didn’t bet), you have options:\n\nOption 1: **Check (0)**  \n- If opponent has a worse hand (like Kx, 9x, 7x, 6x, etc.), they might bluff or value bet a weaker hand.\n- But if they check behind, you win without building pot.\n- Risk: giving free showdown.\n\nOption 2: **Bet (e.g., 100 or more)**  \n- This is **value betting**. Many hands weaker than yours might call: top pair (Kx), middle pairs, ace high, or even gutshot draws.\n- A small bet (e.g., 100) is low risk and could get calls from worse.\n- However, if they have a Jack, they might raise, but still — you want action when ahead.\n\nGiven your straight is very strong and only beaten by Jack-X (which is rare), and considering opponent has been passive, it\'s highly likely you have the best hand.\n\nThus, **betting for value** is correct.\n\nMinimum raise is not enforced