k”.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, we must interpret the valid actions correctly:\n\n- If the opponent raised by 149 on the flop, and you haven\'t acted yet in this round, then:\n   - You cannot check.\n   - You must either fold, call 149, raise to at least 298, or all-in.\n\nSo likely:\n- “-1” = Fold\n- “0” = Call (despite being labeled “Check”)\n- “298” = Minimum raise (149 × 2)\n- Any higher raise up to 19,900\n\nThis is a standard simplification in some AI poker environments.\n\nNow, evaluating your hand:\n- Your hole cards: 2♠, 7♥\n- Board: J♦, 6♣, 5♣\n\nYou have:\n- No pair\n- No draw to a straight: you’d need 3–4 or 8 for an open-ender, but you have 2 and 7. The only straight possibility would be 3-4, but 2-3-4-5-6 — you don’t have 3 or 4. Even then, 7 is too high.\n- You can’t make a flush — you have no clubs; board has two clubs (6♣, 5♣), so a flush draw would require two more clubs — but you have none.\n- You have no overcards or high card strength.\n\nYour hand is 2-high. The best you can hope for is a pair of 7s or 2s — but that would likely still lose.\n\nOpponent’s behavior:\n- Checked pre-flop (likely weak or speculative hand, or trap).\n- Then, on the flop (J♦, 6♣, 5♣), they **raised by 149** — showing strength.\n\nThis flop hits many strong hands:\n- Top pair (JX)\n- Middle pairs\n- Gutshot straights (e.g., 7-8, 4-3)\n- Open-ended straight draws (like 7-4, 8-7)\n- Flush draws (any two clubs)\n\nBut your 2♠, 7♥ gives you:\n- A weak gutshot (if 3 and 4 come, you get 3-4-5-6-7 straight) — but very low probability.\n- Otherwise, nothing.\n\nEven if you hit a 7, opponent may have Jx, 6x, 5x, or better.\nThe 7 is also on board pairing danger — but you have no second card to pair.\n\nConclusion:\n- Your hand is extremely weak.\n- Opponent showed strength by betting 149 on the flop.\n- You have no significant draw, no showdown value.\n- Calling 149 to potentially win a small pot with a weak hand is negative expected value.\n- Raising would be a bluff — but with no