ous draw for straights or flushes yet. Only one club on board (2♣), so flush unlikely. No connected cards suggesting straight draws.  \n- Opponent has shown passive play in pre-flop (checked), suggesting possible weakness or a cautious hand (e.g., mid-pair, overcards, or drawing hand).  \n- Since this is the final hand of the game and you're in the lead (19,900 chips vs opponent likely having ≤20,000), preserving your chip lead is important. However, maximizing value when ahead can also be correct.  \n- Checking keeps the pot small and avoids bloating it with a marginal hand. But a small raise could represent strength and protect against draws (e.g., if opponent has K-x or 2-x, they may improve).  \n- Going all-in (–2) is overly aggressive with such a weak holding; only warranted as a bluff, but given the context (final hand, you’re leading), unnecessary risk.  \n- Folding (–1) is invalid here since you can check.\n\nBest options:\n- **Check (0)**: Safe, sees free turn card, doesn’t commit more chips with weak pair.\n- **Raise to 100**: Small probe bet to gain information or take down the pot if opponent folds weaker hands (like K-x, A-x without pair).\n\nHowever, note: big blind is likely 100 (based on raise minimum), so previous betting round had no bets (both checked). Raising now would be a continuation bet-like move.\n\nBut: your hand has very low showdown value beyond this pair. Overfolding or over-betting risks losing more when behind.\n\nReason:  \nGiven that you are in the lead and hold only a weak pair with poor kickers, the optimal strategy is to **minimize risk** while still playing legally. A small raise might extract value from worse pairs or bluffs, but it also inflates the pot where you’re vulnerable to any improvement by the opponent (e.g., they may have K-2, 2-2, or hit a higher pair on later streets). Since both players checked pre-flop, aggression now may not succeed often enough. **Checking maintains control**, keeps the pot small, and allows fo