le with only one card left. So **you have no straight draw**.\n\nOnly outs: improving to **three sixes** (if another 6 comes), but there's only one 6 left (6♦ already on board, 6♣ in hand, 6♥ on board → wait: 6♥ is on board? Board has 6 of Hearts — yes. So two 6s on board: 6♥ and your 6♣. So one more 6 (6♦) could come. That would give you trips (three of a kind).\n\nBut: board already has **6♥**, so third 6 is 6♦ — only one out: 6♦.\n\nAlso, pairing your 3? 3♦ in hand — another 3 could come (two left: 3♥, 3♠), giving you two pair (6s and 3s). But that’s weak.\n\nSo total decent outs: ~3 (two 3s and one 6) — only 3 outs (~6% chance to improve).\n\nThus, your hand is very weak. But betting won't help much — you can't credibly represent strength.\n\nSince opponent checked through, and you have position, safest move is to **check behind** and avoid losing more chips with a poor hand.\n\nReason:  \nChecking preserves your chip lead and avoids bluffing into potential strength. With only 3 outs and no draws, raising lacks equity. Folding is unnecessary since you can check. All-in is overly aggressive and unjustified.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
